	<annotations>
		<text id="0" type="text/plain" rect="(74.54545454545453, 501.3636363636364, 678.1818181818182, 188.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">1). Task Type: Classification

Target Variable: Categories

Justification: Categories was chosen as target because it is the outcome we want to predict.

Excluded variable: Content and Sender's email are excluded because they contain a lot of unique or unstructured data, which can make the analysis much more complex. </text>
		<text id="1" type="text/plain" rect="(787.727272727273, 499.18181818181824, 352.727272727273, 188.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">2) Root Node Feature: Specific keywords
Under 10-fold cross-validation, the Random Forest model achieved higher performance than the Decision Tree. It obtained 82.1% accuracy, compared with 71.3% accuracy for the Decision Tree. 
</text>
		<text id="2" type="text/plain" rect="(51.785714285714306, 840.3571428571429, 150.0, 56.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">Regression Model:
</text>
		<text id="3" type="text/plain" rect="(94.28571428571433, -182.14285714285717, 211.42857142857127, 56.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">Classification Model: </text>
		<text id="4" type="text/plain" rect="(1177.0, 494.0, 575.0, 215.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">3) An operational manager or advisor could use the predictions and probability scores generated in Stage 5 to identify emails that are likely to be spam and take early action. For example, emails with a high probability of being spam could be automatically moved to the spam folder, while emails with lower confidence could be flagged for manual review. Emails with uncertain predictions could be checked before any action is taken. This helps reduce the risk of missing important emails, saves time, and improves email security.</text>
	</annotations>
